      <INFO number="45021" severity="2">
        <TITLE><![CDATA[Information Gathered from DHCP/Bootp Server]]></TITLE>
        <LAST_UPDATE><![CDATA[2003-07-25T16:48:19Z]]></LAST_UPDATE>
        <PCI_FLAG>0</PCI_FLAG>
        <DIAGNOSIS><![CDATA[DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) and Bootp (Boot Protocol) are widely used protocols for getting network configuration information from a central server. Configuration information may include the network IP address, subnet mask, router addresses, DNS addresses, domain name, hostname, server IP address, server name, DHCP/Bootp relay address, default SMTP/POP3 server addresses, etc. The DHCP protocol itself has no authentication or authorization mechanism and is designed to send out such information to all clients that request it.
<p>
This module makes unicast DHCP Discover requests (or Bootp requests). If the DHCP/Bootp server replies with a broadcast reply and the scanner is not on the same subnet, then the reply will not reach the scanner, and the server will not be detected and hence probed for information. DHCP servers are required per the DHCP protocol specification to respond with unicast replies to our requests; only non-RFC compliant DHCP servers will respond with broadcast replies. 
<P>
If present, DHCP/Bootp relays still make it possible for such broadcast replies to be handed to the scanner. If a relay is present, and it hands the scanner the reply, it too will be detected and reported in the Results section of this report.]]></DIAGNOSIS>
        <CONSEQUENCE><![CDATA[This module probed a DHCP/Bootp server running on this host on UDP port 67 and gathered the information displayed in the Results section of this report. Network configuration information gathered from the DHCP servers can be used by attackers to better map the network for a more damaging future attack. Depending on site policy, disclosure of such network configuration information to unauthorized remote clients may be considered a vulnerability.]]></CONSEQUENCE>
        <SOLUTION><![CDATA[DHCP/Bootp servers should only serve subnets that have intended and authorized DHCP/Bootp clients that depend on them. All unneeded servers should be disabled. Clearly, all inbound requests at the network's perimeter should be blocked by a properly configured firewall (at the perimeter/firewall, block UDP packets destined for port 67 on an internal host and block UDP packets with a source port of 67 leaving the network). This last will limit any mapping-information theft to attackers within the network.]]></SOLUTION>
        <RESULT><![CDATA[Offered IP Address: 0.0.0.0 
Server IP Address: 0.0.0.0 
Relay IP Address: 0.0.0.0 
Client&apos;s Subnet Mask Address: 255.255.255.0 
Routers on Client&apos;s Subnet: 192.168.10.1 
DNS Servers for Client: 8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4]]></RESULT>
      </INFO>
      <INFO number="45038" severity="1">
        <TITLE><![CDATA[Host Scan Time]]></TITLE>
        <LAST_UPDATE><![CDATA[2016-03-18T21:41:40Z]]></LAST_UPDATE>
        <PCI_FLAG>0</PCI_FLAG>
        <DIAGNOSIS><![CDATA[The Host Scan Time is the period of time it takes the scanning engine to perform the vulnerability assessment of a single target host. The Host Scan Time for this host is reported in the Result section below. 
<P>
The Host Scan Time does not have a direct correlation to the Duration time as displayed in the Report Summary section of a scan results report. The Duration is the period of time it takes the service to perform a scan task. The Duration includes the time it takes the service to scan all hosts, which may involve parallel scanning. It also includes the time it takes for a scanner appliance to pick up the scan task and transfer the results back to the service's Secure Operating Center. Further, when a scan task is distributed across multiple scanners, the Duration includes the time it takes to perform parallel host scanning on all scanners.
<P>
For host running the Qualys Windows agent this QID reports the time taken by the agent to collect the host metadata used for the most recent assessment scan.]]></DIAGNOSIS>
        <CONSEQUENCE><![CDATA[N/A]]></CONSEQUENCE>
        <SOLUTION><![CDATA[N/A]]></SOLUTION>
        <RESULT><![CDATA[Scan duration: 662 seconds

Start time: Mon, Jun 20 2016, 17:08:02 GMT

End time: Mon, Jun 20 2016, 17:19:04 GMT]]></RESULT>
      </INFO>
