      <PRACTICE number="38598" severity="2">
        <TITLE><![CDATA[Deprecated Public Key Length]]></TITLE>
        <LAST_UPDATE><![CDATA[2014-09-09T21:26:09Z]]></LAST_UPDATE>
        <PCI_FLAG>0</PCI_FLAG>
        <DIAGNOSIS><![CDATA[NIST has a special publication <A TARGET="_blank" HREF="http://csrc.nist.gov/publications/nistpubs/800-131A/sp800-131A.pdf">SP800-131A</A>in which it has several recommendation regarding cryptographic algorithm and key length use. The recommendation for key length is:<BR>
 - key lengths less then 1024 bits are disallowed, which means they are considered weak and should not be used.<BR>
 - key lengths between 1024 bits and 2047 bits are deprecated<BR>
 - key lengths 2048 and more are approved and safe to use.<BR><BR>]]></DIAGNOSIS>
        <CONSEQUENCE><![CDATA[Since most certificates are issued for one to two years make sure your certificate will not be used after 2013.]]></CONSEQUENCE>
        <SOLUTION><![CDATA[Please obtain a 2048 bit or more public key length certificate from your Certificate Authority.]]></SOLUTION>
