    <CAT value="General remote services" port="67" protocol="udp">
      <PRACTICE number="38289" severity="5" cveid="CVE-2004-0460,CVE-2004-0461">
        <TITLE><![CDATA[ISC DHCPD Hostname Options Logging Buffer Overflow Vulnerability]]></TITLE>
        <LAST_UPDATE><![CDATA[2009-06-11T09:37:01Z]]></LAST_UPDATE>
        <PCI_FLAG>1</PCI_FLAG>
        <CVE_ID_LIST>
          <CVE_ID>
            <ID><![CDATA[CVE-2004-0460]]></ID>
            <URL><![CDATA[http://cve.mitre.org/cgi-bin/cvename.cgi?name=CVE-2004-0460]]></URL>
          </CVE_ID>
          <CVE_ID>
            <ID><![CDATA[CVE-2004-0461]]></ID>
            <URL><![CDATA[http://cve.mitre.org/cgi-bin/cvename.cgi?name=CVE-2004-0461]]></URL>
          </CVE_ID>
        </CVE_ID_LIST>
        <BUGTRAQ_ID_LIST>
          <BUGTRAQ_ID>
            <ID><![CDATA[10590]]></ID>
            <URL><![CDATA[http://www.securityfocus.com/bid/10590]]></URL>
          </BUGTRAQ_ID>
        </BUGTRAQ_ID_LIST>
        <DIAGNOSIS><![CDATA[(Note: The scanner posts this (fairly critical) vulnerability based solely on the detection of a DHCP service running on the target. This is due to the lack of vendor/version information from the DHCP protocol. Please check if the target is indeed running the ISC DHCPD server, and a vulnerable version of it, to see if it needs to be upgraded.)
<P>
ISC DHCPD is prone to a remotely exploitable buffer overflow vulnerability. This issue exists in routines responsible for logging hostname options provided by DHCP clients.
<P>
In particular, the DHCPD logs all DHCP packets, including DISCOVER, OFFER, REQUEST, ACK, and NAK packets. The client-supplied hostname and hostname options will be logged from these packets without sufficient bounds checking. Multiple hostname options may potentially be concatenated to a finite internal buffer resulting in a buffer overflow. To trigger this condition, the hostname and options must contain only ASCII characters.
<P>
This issue is reported to affect ISC DHCPD Versions 3.0.1rc12 and 3.0.1rc13. The vulnerable code exists in previous versions of ISC DHCPD 3, but is believed to be exploitable only in these two releases.]]></DIAGNOSIS>
        <CONSEQUENCE><![CDATA[Successful exploitation could result in execution of arbitrary code in the context of the DHCPD server.]]></CONSEQUENCE>
        <SOLUTION><![CDATA[ISC has addressed this issue with the released of ISC DHCPD Version 3.0.1rc14. Check <A HREF="http://www.isc.org/" TARGET="_blank">ISC's Web site</A> for upgrades.

<P>Virtual Patches:<BR><A HREF="http://www.trendmicro.com/vulnerabilitycontrols"TARGET="_blank">Trend Micro Virtual Patching</A><BR>
Virtual Patch #1000156: ISC DHCP Buffer Overflow Vulnerabilities<BR>
Virtual Patch #1000709: ISC DHCPD Hostname Options Logging Buffer Overflow Vulnerability<BR>]]></SOLUTION>
        <RESULT><![CDATA[Detected service DHCP_or_Bootp_Server and os Ubuntu / Linux 2.6.x]]></RESULT>
      </PRACTICE>
    </CAT>
  </PRACTICES>
