          </EXPLOITABILITY>
        </CORRELATION>
        <RESULT><![CDATA[Tested on port 21 with an injected SYN/RST offset by 16 bytes.
Tested on port 23 with an injected SYN/RST offset by 16 bytes.]]></RESULT>
      </VULN>
      <VULN number="82058" severity="2" cveid="CVE-2004-0790,CAN-2004-0791,CAN-2004-1060">
        <TITLE><![CDATA[ICMP Based TCP Reset Denial of Service Vulnerability]]></TITLE>
        <LAST_UPDATE><![CDATA[2013-06-07T17:04:02Z]]></LAST_UPDATE>
        <PCI_FLAG>0</PCI_FLAG>
        <CVE_ID_LIST>
          <CVE_ID>
            <ID><![CDATA[CVE-2004-0790]]></ID>
            <URL><![CDATA[http://cve.mitre.org/cgi-bin/cvename.cgi?name=CVE-2004-0790]]></URL>
          </CVE_ID>
          <CVE_ID>
            <ID><![CDATA[CAN-2004-0791]]></ID>
            <URL><![CDATA[http://cve.mitre.org/cgi-bin/cvename.cgi?name=CAN-2004-0791]]></URL>
          </CVE_ID>
          <CVE_ID>
            <ID><![CDATA[CAN-2004-1060]]></ID>
            <URL><![CDATA[http://cve.mitre.org/cgi-bin/cvename.cgi?name=CAN-2004-1060]]></URL>
          </CVE_ID>
        </CVE_ID_LIST>
        <BUGTRAQ_ID_LIST>
          <BUGTRAQ_ID>
            <ID><![CDATA[13124]]></ID>
            <URL><![CDATA[http://www.securityfocus.com/bid/13124]]></URL>
          </BUGTRAQ_ID>
        </BUGTRAQ_ID_LIST>
        <DIAGNOSIS><![CDATA[The target host is vulnerable to a denial of service condition. The TCP stack present on the host allows an ICMP hard-error packet to reset an established TCP connection that the packet identifies. An example ICMP hard error (defined in the IETF RFCs) is the ICMP message &quot;fragmentation required, but Do-Not-Fragment bit is set&quot;.]]></DIAGNOSIS>
        <CONSEQUENCE><![CDATA[Since ICMP packets can be spoofed, attackers can exploit this issue by guessing the IP address and port numbers of a TCP connection established on the host, and then resetting these connections simply by sending an ICMP hard-error packet.]]></CONSEQUENCE>
        <SOLUTION><![CDATA[HP has released an updated advisory <A HREF="http://h20000.www2.hp.com/bizsupport/TechSupport/Document.jsp?lang=en&cc=us&objectID=c00576017" TARGET="_blank">HPSBUX01164</A> to address this issue.
<P>
Microsoft Security Bulletin <A HREF="http://www.microsoft.com/technet/security/Bulletin/MS05-019.mspx" TARGET="_blank">MS05-019</A>.
<P>
Symantec has released an advisory <A HREF="http://securityresponse.symantec.com/avcenter/security/Content/2005.05.02.html" TARGET="_blank">SYM05-008</A>
<P>
Cisco has released an advisory <A HREF="http://tools.cisco.com/security/center/content/CiscoSecurityAdvisory/cisco-sa-20050412-icmp" TARGET="_blank">cisco-sa-20050412-icmp</A> and fixes to address these vulnerabilities.
<P>
A workaround is to block ICMP hard-error packets using a firewall.
<P>Patch:<BR>
Following are links for downloading patches to fix the vulnerabilities:
<P> <A HREF="http://h20000.www2.hp.com/bizsupport/TechSupport/Document.jsp?lang=en&cc=us&objectID=c00576017" TARGET="_blank">HPSBUX01164: HP-UX</A><P> <A HREF="http://www.microsoft.com/technet/security/Bulletin/MS05-019.mspx" TARGET="_blank">MS05-019: Windows</A><P> <A HREF="http://securityresponse.symantec.com/avcenter/security/Content/2005.05.02.html" TARGET="_blank">SYM05-008</A><P> <A HREF="http://tools.cisco.com/security/center/content/CiscoSecurityAdvisory/cisco-sa-20050412-icmp" TARGET="_blank">cisco-sa-20050412-icmp</A>]]></SOLUTION>
